{
  "seq": 20,
  "kind": "turn",
  "at": "2026-10-02T11:13:08.395Z",
  "chatId": "2026-10-02@08h03m21s586ms",
  "line": "Sable, Mirek: a vault guarded by knights who swear the dragon is dead. Who on the inside can we buy, and what do they want?",
  "ok": true,
  "problems": [],
  "replied": true,
  "replies": [
    {
      "messageId": 6,
      "speaker": "Sable",
      "text": "\"You're thinking like a professional. I like that.\" Sable doesn't smile, but there's a ghost of approval in her eyes. She leans back, the worn leather of her coat creaking. \"The Order of the Silver Lance doesn't take coin from the likes of us. Their knights are too proud for that, and their keepers are too paranoid. We're not buying a man; we're buying a secret.\"\nShe holds up a hand, counting off on her fingers. \"Their Master-of-Arms, a man named Garreth. He's got a daughter in the Noble District who owes more to the ledger-houses than the crown does. He doesn't need gold—he needs her debt erased. The knights won't pay it. But we can.\"\n\"The other angle,\" Sable continues, her voice dropping lower, \"is the vault's keeper. A man who knows the wards better than anyone, and who's been watching them rust for ten years. He's old, forgotten by the knights he serves, and he's got a grudge that's been simmering since the day they dragged that hoard down into the dark.\"\nShe looks between you and Mirek. \"We approach them differently. Garreth wants his daughter free. The keeper wants to see the Order's lie crumble. Both can be bought, just not with coin.\""
    },
    {
      "messageId": 7,
      "speaker": "Mirek",
      "text": "*Mirek's fingers have stopped their restless dance with the pick. He's gone still, eyes narrowed as he processes Sable's words, his mind already mapping the vault in the dark.*\n\"A grudge doesn't buy a key, and a debt doesn't stop a sword.\" *His voice is flat, cautious.* \"Garreth's daughter is a lever, not a door. If he's paranoid enough to keep a secret this big, he'll be paranoid enough to suspect the people paying it off. And a forgotten keeper? Those are the ones who spot you coming from a mile away because they're the only ones actually watching.\"\n*He looks toward Vaelrith, then back to you.* \"Sable's right about one thing—it's not about coin. But we're missing something. A vault warded against magic and lockpicks isn't just a matter of turning a key and knowing a guard's schedule. It's about the wards themselves. Someone wrote them. Someone maintains them.\" *A small, sharp movement of his hand suggests a lock being picked.* \"That's where we'll find the weak point. Not in a man's greed or his grudge, but in the logic of the lock itself.\"\n*His gaze settles on you, calculating.* \"You're the noble in this crew. Your name carries weight in places our names don't exist. We get us close enough to the chapterhouse, and you're our way in. Not as a thief, but as someone who belongs there. Then we're inside the wards, and the rest... that's on me.\""
    },
    {
      "messageId": 8,
      "speaker": "Vaelrith",
      "text": "*\"You talk of lords and names and levers,\"* Vaelrith's voice rumbles like a distant storm, the sound filling the cavern. The dragon's clouded eye catches the torchlight. *\"You think the name Nightriver will open a door? Names are just words, and words are just breath. The knights care for nothing but their own glory, and the Order's vaults are built on pride.\"*\nThe dragon shifts slightly, the sound of scale on stone like grinding rock. *\"You seek a weak point? Every lie has one. The knights' claim that I am dead is their strength, but it is also their weakness. They believe the hoard is safe because they believe I am gone. They have grown soft in their certainty.\"*\nA thin plume of smoke curls from Vaelrith's nostril. *\"The vault's wards are strong, yes. But every ward has a key, and every key is held by someone. You need not buy the keeper or the master-at-arms. You need only find the one who placed the wards in the first place—a man who may have forgotten them, or whose heirs still hold the secret.\"*\nThe dragon's voice drops to a low, knowing rumble. *\"And if you cannot find the key, you will create a new one. A distraction. A crisis that will force the Order to open the vault to prove its prize is still safe. The knights are vain; they cannot resist the urge to show off their trophy to the world.\"*\n*\"The question is not who we can buy,\"* Vaelrith continues, the good eye narrowing. *\"The question is what you will do to make them open the door. Name your price, and tell me how you will breach their sanctum.\"*"
    }
  ],
